     "content": "This is an **exceptionally strong, top-of-market offer** for an L6 Product Manager at Google. You should not walk away under any circumstances. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Breakdown & Year-by-Year Math\n\nGoogle's new-hire equity typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (most commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**). \n\nUnder the standard **33 / 33 / 22 / 12** schedule, your annualized compensation looks like this:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **RSU Vesting** | $442,200 (33%) | $442,200 (33%) | $294,800 (22%) | $160,800 (12%) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Estimated Total Comp** | **~$954,200** | **~$754,200** | **~$606,800\\*** | **~$472,800\\*** |\n\n*\\*Years 3 and 4 will be supplemented by annual equity refreshes (typically granted annually, vesting over 4 years).*\n\n#### How this compares to Meta IC6:\n* **Current Meta Comp:** ~$476,000\u2013$526,000/yr.\n* **Google 4-Year Average (flat):** ~$697,000/yr (including sign-on).\n* **Year 1 Increase:** You will be making nearly **double** your current Meta compensation in your first year.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Analysis of Offer Components\n\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** The posted band is $183k\u2013$271k. At $260k, you are already at the **87th percentile of the band**.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($200,000):** This is unusually high for L6 (standard ranges are typically $50k\u2013$100k). It strongly compensates for any unvested equity or timing gap you leave behind at Meta.\n* **Equity ($1,340,000):** Median L6 equity grant sizes are typically $600k\u2013$900k over 4 years. A $1.34M grant puts you at the absolute top tier for L6.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept or Negotiate?\n\n* **Do not walk away:** This offer significantly beats your current compensation and typical L6 market benchmarks.\n* **Negotiate lightly with a \"closing\" ask:** Because you have no competing offers and Google is already your first choice, you do not want to overplay your hand or sound ungrounded. However, in Big Tech, making **one polite, targeted counter-ask tied to an immediate acceptance** is standard practice and carries very low risk.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For and How Much\n\nSince your base and equity are already near the top of the band, focus on a modest adjustment and tie it to signing immediately:\n\n1. **Base Salary:** Ask for **$270,000** (rounding up to the top of the posted $271,000 band).\n2. **Equity:** Ask for **$1,400,000** (a modest ~$60k increase on a $1.34M grant).\n\n#### Recommended Script / Talking Points:\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this together. I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and the scope of this role\u2014Google is my clear top choice. The package is very strong, but to make this an immediate 'yes' without hesitation, I\u2019d love to see if we can get the base to **$270,000** (closer to the top of the posted range) and round the initial equity grant to **$1,400,000**. If you can make those adjustments, I'm ready to sign right away.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Details to Clarify Before Signing\n\n* **Sign-On Payment & Clawback:** Confirm if the $200k is paid in a single lump sum in month one or split across Year 1 and Year 2, and clarify the clawback terms (typically pro-rated over 12 months).\n* **Vesting Schedule & Cadence:** Confirm whether your grant follows the 33/33/22/12 schedule and vests monthly or quarterly.\n* **Tax Planning:** Because Year 1 total comp will approach ~$950k, Google's default supplemental tax withholding on RSUs (22%) will likely under-withhold relative to your top marginal tax bracket. Plan for quarterly estimated taxes accordingly.",
